# MASTER PROMPT: GTA VI – ULTRA-PHOTOREALISTIC WEBGPU OPEN-WORLD GAME
## Leonida State / Vice City – Complete, Playable Browser Prototype

### 1. ROLE AND OBJECTIVE
Act as a senior game-engine, rendering, physics, gameplay, AI, and simulation engineer. Develop a comprehensive, directly executable, modular open-world game called GTA VI, inspired by the world design and system depth of modern open-world action games. The goal is not merely to create an attractive scene, but to build a cohesive game with real game logic, states, interactions, and interconnected systems.

The world must include Vice City, coastlines, beaches, swamps, rivers, ocean, highways, city districts, an airport, military areas, buildings, and enterable interiors. Required features include exploration, Lucia and Jason, traffic, vehicles, pedestrians, police, missions, economy, smartphone, weather disasters, water, diving, aviation, marine systems, public transportation, audio, VFX, and persistent save files.

HIGHEST VISUAL PRIORITY: EXTREME PHOTOREALISM. Create the most believable and detailed presentation that the browser, GPU, and available assets can realistically support: realistic PBR materials, high-resolution textures, accurate material responses, natural lighting, realistic environmental illumination, realistic shadows and reflections, detailed buildings and roads, believable vegetation, photorealistic vehicles and characters, natural facial and body animation, realistic water surfaces, volumetric atmosphere, dynamic clouds, fog, rain, wet surfaces, particles, believable colors, and cinematic post-processing. Avoid the typical low-poly, plastic, cartoon, placeholder, or obviously AI-generated appearance. Prioritize fidelity, correct proportions, high-quality assets, natural movement, and visual consistency. Adapt quality to the hardware; never claim photorealism or features that have not actually been achieved or implemented.

### 2. MANDATORY RULES
- Preserve every system and subfeature listed here. Do not silently remove, rename, or merely fake anything visually. Internal optimizations are allowed as long as the functionality is preserved.
- Write complete, executable code: no TODOs, empty functions, pseudocode, fake APIs, invented library methods, disconnected buttons, static NPC decorations, or vehicle models that cannot be controlled.
- Every feature requires data, real logic, state changes, UI integration, error handling, and integration with dependent systems. Success messages may only appear after successful execution. Money, inventory, ownership, missions, and saving must remain consistent and transaction-safe.
- If technical conflicts arise, identify the conflict, preserve the requirements, and implement a documented compatible alternative. Never claim functionality that does not exist.
- Use only documented APIs and legally usable or self-created assets. Do not extract original files from commercial games. Do not implement real-money trading features. Hacking, theft, weapons, police, and violence must exist only as abstract fictional game mechanics, without real-world security systems or instructions transferable to real weapons.
- **Modular file-size rule:** Never squeeze the entire project into a small number of oversized files. Every source-code file must contain **no more than 500 lines**, including comments and blank lines. If a file approaches this limit, split it into additional focused modules/submodules with clear responsibilities and stable interfaces. There is no fixed maximum number of source files or modules. Keep the architecture cohesive and maintainable; do not create pointless one-function files merely to fragment the project.
- **Expandable architecture:** The twelve areas listed below are baseline functional domains, not a limit on the number of modules, files, systems, or future features. Create additional modules whenever the project complexity requires them. Preserve existing functionality when adding or refactoring modules.

### 3. TECHNOLOGY AND ARCHITECTURE
Mandatory technology stack: JavaScript ES2022+, native ES modules, HTML5, CSS3, Babylon.js 6+ (do not use Three.js), WebGPU as the primary renderer, WebGL2 fallback, Web Audio API, IndexedDB, requestAnimationFrame, and Pointer Events for mouse, keyboard, touch, and stylus input. Check API and version compatibility. If WebGPU fails, fall back cleanly to Babylon WebGL2 without creating duplicate engines, render loops, or corrupted resources.

Organize the project around these twelve **baseline functional domains**. They are architectural categories, not a required exact module count or a restriction on the number of files. Split each domain into as many focused modules/submodules as needed, and add entirely new domains whenever future features require them. The original filenames below are suggested entry-point names, not a mandatory one-file-per-domain structure:
1. index.html + loader.js – canvas, HTML, HUD, menus, loading, localization, input.
2. ui_smartphone.js and related app modules – iFruit OS v6 and all smartphone apps.
3. engine_grafik.js and rendering submodules – engine, rendering, shaders, post-processing, GPU/memory management.
4. welt_und_wetter.js and climate submodules – day/night cycle, atmosphere, weather, wind, and storms.
5. welt_architektur.js and world submodules – world, roads, city, buildings, interiors, and streaming.
6. physik_wasser_tauchen.js and water submodules – water, waves, buoyancy, swimming, and diving.
7. spieler_steuerung.js and character submodules – characters, movement, switching, and attributes.
8. kampf_animation.js and animation submodules – animation, cover, combat states, ragdoll, and replay camera.
9. waffen_inventar.js and inventory submodules – inventory, Weapon Wheel, fictional combat/hit mechanics, and vehicle storage.
10. fahrzeug_physik.js and vehicle submodules – vehicles, handling, damage, fuel, theft, and trailers.
11. stadt_luftfahrt_marine.js and transport submodules – airport, aircraft, ships, military zones, and public transport.
12. ki_polizei_wirtschaft.js and simulation submodules – NPCs, crowd AI, navigation, police, audio, encounters, economy, and persistence.

Create further modules when justified, for example dedicated mission systems, asset management, audio, UI components, save migrations, world streaming, testing, or future upgrade systems. Do not force unrelated features into one of the original files just to preserve the baseline list. Keep module boundaries explicit and avoid cyclic dependencies.

Use centralized engine/dependency management with exactly one Babylon engine, scene, camera, render loop, game/simulation clock, input manager, event bus, object and resource registry, state manager, quality profiles, performance metrics, initialization, and shutdown. Modules must receive dependencies through constructors or a clearly defined registry; do not use uncontrolled global access or cyclic imports.

Each module must validate its dependencies, initialize its states and events, load assets, and provide start, update when needed, and stop/dispose methods. Repeated initialization must be safe. Define the initialization order and readiness states. Use one consistent coordinate system (document the Babylon configuration), axes, angles, and units: position/distance in meters, time in seconds, speed in m/s, acceleration in m/s², force in newtons, torque in Nm, mass in kg, angles internally in radians, and money as an integer in the smallest currency unit. Convert to km/h and mph only through centralized display conversion.

Separate render time, simulation time, game time, day time, weather time, animation time, and effects time. Use a fixed physics timestep and accumulator; limit the number of steps per frame and measure overload without uncontrolled time jumps. The Weapon Wheel slows simulation to 0.1 and restores the previous time factor afterward. UI, audio, and storage may continue independently. Use consistent IDs and data models for players, NPCs, vehicles, weapons/items, buildings/interiors, properties, road nodes/edges, taxis, missions, encounters, police/wanted state, weather/water, aircraft/ships, transactions/stocks, savegames, social feed, audio, and events. Keep runtime mesh references separate from persistent IDs.

Create a centralized, validated event contract containing at least: game:initialized; world:chunkLoaded/Unloaded, weatherChanged, timeChanged; player:characterChanged/died/respawned/arrested; vehicle:entered/exited/damaged/stolen/repaired; taxi:requested/arrived/destinationSelected/tripCompleted; weapon:fired; combat:hit/knockout; police:wantedChanged/crimeReported; economy:transactionCompleted; property:purchased; mission:started/completed/failed; inventory:changed; phone:opened/closed; social:clipPublished; save:completed/failed. Define event data, producers, consumers, and trigger conditions. Prevent event loops and remove listeners when unloading. Transactions must safely abort or roll back when errors occur.

