### 6. MODULE 3 – engine_grafik.js
Initialize Babylon WebGPU asynchronously and fall back to WebGL2 if initialization fails. Keep scenes, materials, shaders, and effects compatible; replace or disable unsupported features, clean up resources, and run only one render loop. Provide Low/Medium/High/Ultra graphics profiles with dynamic resolution, shadows, LOD, vegetation, NPCs, particles, clouds, and post-processing. Profiles must be changeable at runtime and always account for hardware limits.

For maximum photorealism: PBR BaseColor/Metallic/Roughness/Normal/AO/Emissive maps; realistic glass, metal, fabric, wood, asphalt, and water responses; wet materials; reflections; and natural lighting changes. Post-processing: ACES, HDR bloom, depth of field with a real focus point, controlled chromatic aberration, motion blur based on movement data, vignette, and state transitions. Use volumetric/approximated 3D clouds, fog/ray marching, swamp ground fog, light scattering, and quality/distance limits. Use parallax interior mapping for non-enterable windows with facade coordinates, room depth/divisions, material and lighting variation, reproducible rooms, and night lighting; do not confuse these with real enterable interiors.

Implement dynamic shadows using Babylon ShadowGenerator, a suitable ESM variant and/or CSM, up to 4096² only when hardware permits. Document the actual active method, filter, bias, splits, distance, and object types. Use distance-based LOD, frustum/occlusion culling, instancing, region streaming, unloading, and visibility filters. Do not use stale occlusion results that permanently hide objects. Manage textures, buffers, render targets, shaders, meshes, materials, and particles with clear release rules. Handle tab switching, resizing, context loss, restarts, and scene changes.

