### 16. GLOBAL GAME START, ECONOMY, AND GAME-OVER RULES
First game start: initialize engine, load required assets, register main world, build Star Island region/villa, create character, connect camera/HUD, validate state, and enable input. Distinguish a new game from an existing save being resumed.

Starting point is the courtyard of the Star Island luxury villa.

Death/WASTED: register once, correctly lock controls, play game-over sequence, deduct 10% of cash (maximum 5,000 in-game currency; never allow a negative balance), apply weapon-loss rules, respawn at the hospital, restore character/camera/controls, and save when appropriate. Apply the penalty only once per death event.

Arrest/BUSTED: register once, reset wanted status, calculate and deduct bail, remove or impound prohibited weapons/ammunition, respawn at the prison or a valid PD point, update inventory/camera/state consistently, and save when appropriate. Define behavior when funds are insufficient; never apply duplicate deductions.

Tuning: engine power, top speed, torque, suspension, spoiler, paint/metallic/pearlescent effects, wheels, and other suitable parts. Parameters must actually change handling or appearance. Verify price before purchase; apply changes only after a successful transaction.

Wardrobe: clothing/outfits, tattoos, and hairstyles appropriate to the character assets actually available, with valid state and persistence.

ATMs: deposits/withdrawals/balance checks only near registered ATMs. Balances must never become negative; log every transaction.

Robberies/hostages: shops and banks with mission, alarm, NPCs, witnesses, escape, loot, police, optional fictional hacking, success/failure/cancel states, and payout. Hostage control must be a real gameplay and NPC state, not a static animation.

Cargo: trailers, hitches, shifting cargo, cargo weight/condition, winches, and cargo interactions. Cargo should affect center of mass and handling wherever possible.

### 17. AUDIO, VFX, PERFORMANCE
Audio voices for engines, turbo, tires, exhaust, and sirens must use simulation values; control volume, priorities, and transitions. Particles: sparks, tire smoke, rain, splashes, water mist, sea spray, bubbles, dust, muzzle/explosion/impact effects; include resource budgets, lifetimes, and recycling. Decals for impacts/traces must account for surface, geometry, lifetime/persistence, and cleanup. Clothing/hair must use available cloth/hair physics or a documented simplified solution; do not make false simulation claims.

Target platforms: powerful desktop PCs, integrated GPUs, compatible Apple Silicon iPads, WebGPU, and WebGL2. Do not guarantee 60 FPS universally. Use 60 FPS / approximately 16.67 ms as a target budget; measure actual CPU update time, render/frame time, frame variance, active entities, draw calls, visible objects, particles, physics, and measurable memory.

Simulation levels: near = full interaction/animation/collision/audio; medium = simplified animation, lower update frequency, perception, and physics; far = statistical crowd/traffic states and minimal updates. Preserve states when switching levels. Adaptive quality must gradually reduce post-processing, shadows, particles, distant details, remote simulation frequency, and render resolution under load; core gameplay logic must remain active. Set centralized limits for NPCs, vehicles, particles, projectiles, decals, audio sources, temporary meshes, chunks, replay data, and render targets.

### 18. ROBUSTNESS, SECURITY, AND TESTING
Validate positions, time, money, IDs, inventory quantities, weather, angles, speeds, missions, and saves. Prevent NaN, Infinity, invalid indices, and unbounded states. Event-handler errors must not crash the entire game uncontrollably; log the event, module, object, state, and cause. Handle critical errors in a controlled way. Browser cases: resize, tab switching/inactivity, focus loss, audio permission, storage failure, context loss, missing WebGPU, asset errors, and interrupted simulation. Prevent duplicate transactions/penalties/rewards/spawns, lost vehicle ownership, invalid accounts/inventories, and corrupted database upgrades.

Test each module individually for initialization, invalid configuration, missing dependencies, duplicate initialization, update, stop, cleanup, and error handling. Integration tests must include at least these flows:
A. Game startup: WebGPU or WebGL2 fallback, assets, villa, controls.
B. Vehicle: enter, drive, brake, steer, exit, correct HUD values.
C. Rain: wetness and hydroplaning affect grip.
D. Taxi: request, approach, board, destination route.
E. Character switching: separate states, controls, camera, HUD.
F. Smartphone/economy: app states and real transaction.
G. Combat: attack, hit validation, and NPC state.
H. Police: perception, witnesses, wanted level, pursuit, arrest.
I. Airport: valid flight phases and coordinated runways.
J. Diving: dive, oxygen, underwater movement, surface.
K. Saving: save, reload, validated restoration.
L. Recovery: optional asset errors, database abort, and browser pause without inconsistent state.

